---
// [WEB-LEGAL-FOLK-1 2026-09-27] /refunds rebuilt to the owner-approved mockup
// design/legal-pages/saathum-refunds-mock.src.html. OWNER: "exact replica of
// what you have shown me, not an inspired version." Replaces the
// [SAATHUM-REFUNDS-1] layouts/Content.astro version — same policy text,
// word-for-word, on the new folk shell.
//
// [REFUND-POLICY-WEB-1 2026-09-28] OWNER DECISION: no automatic refunds.
// Cancellation window is now per event type — havan/puja: at least 24 hours
// before start; satsang/sermon/meditation: at least 3 days before start — each
// with its own plain-English reason (team + priest arrangements/travel/
// samagri for havan & puja; venue booking + speaker travel for the talks).
// The window numbers here MUST match lib/eventTypes.ts's refundWindowHours
// (24 / 72) — do not hardcode a different number without updating both.
//
// NOTE FOR AI:
//  - Shell + CSS: layouts/LegalFolk.astro (props documented there) + its
//    styles/legal-folk.css. Same conventions as pages/how-it-works.astro:
//    Base chrome={false}, `.folk-site[data-design="saathum-reference-v5"]`,
//    SiteHeader/SiteFooter `folk`. Do not re-derive the shell here.
//  - This page supplies only content props (title/lead/updated/sticker/toc/
//    prose) — LegalFolk.astro builds the PublicContent SEO object from them.
//  - Emails stay plain text "support (@) saathum.com", never mailto.
//  - No company/entity name here (CLAUDE.md "THERE IS NO COMPANY") — this
//    page names no entity and that stays true after this edit.
export const prerender = true;
import LegalFolk from '../layouts/LegalFolk.astro';
import { publicImage } from '../lib/config';

const toc = [
  { id: 'price', label: 'What you see is what you pay' },
  { id: 'cancel', label: 'Cancelling your booking' },
  { id: 'request', label: 'How to request a refund' },
  { id: 'refund-paid', label: 'How your refund is paid' },
  { id: 'we-cancel', label: "If we cancel or can't perform your event" },
  { id: 'missed', label: 'No automatic refunds for missed attendance' },
  { id: 'outcome', label: 'No refunds based on the outcome' },
  { id: 'disputes', label: 'Wrong or duplicate payments' },
  { id: 'rights', label: 'Your rights' },
];
const ontimeImg = publicImage('/assets/saathum-how-it-works/ontime.webp', { width: 320, fit: 'scale-down' });
---

<LegalFolk
  title="Refunds & Cancellations"
  titleHtml="Refunds &amp; <em>Cancellations</em>"
  eyebrow="Legal"
  lead="What you see is what you pay. Cancel with enough notice — 24 hours for a havan or puja, 3 days for a satsang, sermon or meditation — and we refund you in full."
  description="How cancellations and refunds work on Saa Thum — the notice period for each event type, why it exists, and how to request a refund with your UPI transaction number."
  image="/og/refunds.png"
  updated="2026-09-30"
  helpHref="/help/billing/refunds"
  sticker={{ src: '/assets/saathum-editorial-stickers-v2/refunds-sticker-v2.png', width: 1376, height: 1143, alt: 'A brass bowl of coins with a scroll and a lotus — refunds.' }}
  stamp={{ src: '/assets/saathum-how-it-works/ontime.webp', width: 443, height: 494 }}
  toc={toc}
  railCard={{
    title: 'Keep your UTR handy',
    html: 'Your 12-digit UPI transaction number is how we find your payment. <a href="/help/billing/refunds">Read the help centre</a>',
    image: { src: '/assets/saathum-how-it-works/link.webp', width: 384, height: 640 },
  }}
>
  <p>
    This policy explains how cancellations and refunds work for the pujas, havans, satsangs,
    sermons and meditation sessions you book on Saa Thum. It sits alongside our
    <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="price"><span class="lg-n">1.</span>What you see is what you pay</h2>
  <p>
    The price shown on an event is the full price you pay. There are no tokens, wallets, credits
    or extra charges added at checkout.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cancel"><span class="lg-n">2.</span>Cancelling your booking</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Refunds are not automatic, and there are no refunds for cancelling after your event's
    notice period, or for simply missing it.</strong> How much notice you need to give depends on
    what you booked:
  </p>
  <div class="lg-tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Event type</th>
          <th>Cancel at least</th>
          <th>Why we need the notice</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Havan &amp; Puja</strong></td>
          <td>24 hours before the start</td>
          <td>
            By then our team has made arrangements, and often travelled to a remote location; the
            priest has given his valuable time and travelled to perform it; and the havan samagri,
            flowers and puja materials have already been bought for you.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Satsang, Sermon &amp; Meditation</strong></td>
          <td>3 days before the start</td>
          <td>
            A hall or venue has been booked for the gathering or meditation, and the preacher or
            teacher has often travelled from far away — which is why these need more notice.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="lg-note">
    <div>
      <span class="lg-tag">No automatic refunds</span>
      <p>
        Cancel within your event's notice period above and you get a <strong>full refund</strong> of
        the amount you paid. Cancellations made after that window are not refunded — and neither is
        simply not showing up.
      </p>
    </div>
    <img src={ontimeImg} alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="443" height="494" />
  </div>

  <h2 id="request"><span class="lg-n">3.</span>How to request a refund</h2>
  <p>
    Refunds are not automatic — you need to send us a request. Email
    support (@) saathum.com or use our
    <a href="/contact">contact form</a> and include:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Your full name, and the email or phone number you booked with.</li>
    <li>The event you booked and its scheduled date and time.</li>
    <li><strong>Your 12-digit UPI transaction number</strong> (also called the UTR or UPI reference number) for the payment.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The UPI transaction number is how we locate your payment, so we can't process a refund
    without it. You'll find it in your UPI app (Google Pay, PhonePe, Paytm, BHIM or your bank's
    app) by opening the payment in your transaction history, and in your bank's payment SMS.
  </p>

  <h2 id="refund-paid"><span class="lg-n">4.</span>How your refund is paid</h2>
  <p>
    Once we've located your payment and confirmed your cancellation was made within your event's
    notice period above, we refund the full amount to the UPI account you paid from. We'll let
    you know by email when the refund has been sent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="we-cancel"><span class="lg-n">5.</span>If we cancel or can't perform your event</h2>
  <p>
    If we cancel your event, or can't perform it, you get a full refund regardless of notice.
    Send us a request the same way, with your 12-digit UPI transaction number, and we'll refund
    you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="missed"><span class="lg-n">6.</span>No automatic refunds for missed attendance</h2>
  <p>
    For a havan or puja, the ritual and its sankalp are still performed even if you couldn't join,
    so it is not refunded — your video is sent to you when the ritual finishes. For a satsang,
    sermon or meditation session, the event still takes place at its scheduled time whether or not
    you were free to attend, so a missed session is not refunded either — your video is sent to
    you when it finishes. There are no automatic or partial refunds for missed attendance of any
    kind.
  </p>

  <!-- [SAATHUM-NO-OUTCOME-REFUND-1 2026-09-30] OWNER: no refund for dissatisfaction with a ritual's outcome. -->
  <h2 id="outcome"><span class="lg-n">7.</span>No refunds based on the outcome</h2>
  <p>
    The outcome of a puja, havan, satsang, sermon or meditation is a matter of faith. It can't be promised or measured. Our pujaris and team perform every ritual sincerely and in full, so we don't give refunds because of how you feel about the result. If we could not perform your ritual, you get a full refund.
  </p>

  <!-- [WEB-REFRAME-1 2026-09-29] OWNER: charawa is not returned/refunded; weather delays. -->
  <h2 id="charawa"><span class="lg-n">8.</span>Charawa and weather delays</h2>
  <p>
    What you offer at the altar — chadhava, vastra, coconut, flowers or dakshina — is given to
    the deity during your puja. By tradition it stays at the temple, so charawa is not returned
    or refunded once the puja has been performed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many of our temples are high in the Himalayas. Snow, landslides, heavy rain and other natural
    events can delay your video or prasad. Your ritual has still been performed in your name, so a
    weather delay is not a reason for a refund. We tell you about any delay on WhatsApp and by
    email, and send the video and prasad as soon as we can.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disputes"><span class="lg-n">9.</span>Wrong or duplicate payments</h2>
  <p>
    If you think you were charged by mistake or paid twice, email
    support (@) saathum.com with the 12-digit UPI transaction
    number for each payment. Please contact us before raising a dispute with your bank — it's
    faster, and we'll sort it out directly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rights"><span class="lg-n">10.</span>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing in this policy limits any rights you have under applicable consumer protection law.
  </p>
</LegalFolk>
